1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,980
Picture yourself sitting at home in a quiet reading nook, ignoring the world around you, engrossed in a tale.
想象一下，你坐在家里一个安静的阅读角，对周遭的一切都视而不见，沉浸在一个故事里。

2
00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:17,330
You read the blurb, instantly became intrigued and now you’re in the middle of an absolute page-turner.
你读了书籍简介，瞬间被勾起了兴趣，此刻正在读一本让人欲罢不能的好书。

3
00:00:17,330 --> 00:00:26,560
But, there’s a hot debate – physical books vs. e-books vs. audiobooks.
但一直以来都有一个激烈的争论——纸质书、电子书和有声书，哪种更好？

4
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:31,750
Does the device you’re using to read affect your reaction to the book?
你用来阅读的载体，是否会影响你对这本书的感受？

5
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:43,880
Generally, reading uses several areas of the brain. There’s attention span, reasoning, reading fluency, memory and language comprehension.
通常来说，阅读会调动大脑的多个区域，包括注意力持续时间、逻辑推理、阅读流畅度、记忆力以及语言理解力。

6
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:52,760
Reading is known to strengthen communicative ability, vocabulary and increase emotional intelligence and social perception.
众所周知，阅读能提升沟通能力、扩充词汇量，还能增强情商和社会洞察力。

7
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:56,970
So, whichever way you’re reading, there are definitely benefits.
因此，无论你选择哪种阅读方式，都必然能从中获益。

8
00:00:56,970 --> 00:01:04,180
But, let’s look at the pros to reading with your eyes – that’s physical books and e-books.
不过，我们来看看用眼阅读的好处——这里指的是纸质书和电子书。

9
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:14,150
They can help to retain information better. This is because when you can actually see the words, your attention is held more closely.
这种阅读方式能帮助人们更好地记住信息，因为当你能直观看到文字时，注意力会更加集中。

10
00:01:14,150 --> 00:01:26,680
Add to this the fact that with physical books you can go back and find any part you missed, especially if your mind wanders, which it likely will at some point or other.
此外，读纸质书的时候，你可以随时翻回去查看错过的内容，尤其是在你走神的时候——这种情况或多或少总会发生。

11
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:30,000
On the other hand, there’s the audiobook.
另一方面，有声书也有其独特之处。

12
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:40,460
Headphones in, you’re switched off from life and the story really comes alive, almost like watching a film – in your head.
戴上耳机，你便与现实生活隔绝开来，故事仿佛在脑海中活了起来，就像在脑袋里面看一场电影。

13
00:01:40,460 --> 00:01:51,200
From a scientific perspective, listening to an audiobook is likely to help you develop a greater sense of empathy as you hear the emotion of the narrator.
从科学角度来看，听有声书时，你能听到朗读者的情绪起伏，这有助于培养更强的共情能力。

14
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:55,580
We can more easily understand inflection and intonation.
我们也能更轻松地捕捉到语气的抑扬顿挫。

15
00:01:55,580 --> 00:02:04,490
Hearing the story engages different parts of the brain, heightening the intensity and imagery, making you enjoy it more.
听故事调动的是大脑的不同区域，能增强故事的感染力和画面感，让你更投入地享受阅读。

16
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:13,550
Yet, going back to attention span, with an audiobook it’s true that it’s much harder to go back and listen again.
但回到注意力的问题上，有声书确实存在一个弊端——想要倒回去重听某部分内容，操作起来会麻烦得多。

17
00:02:13,550 --> 00:02:20,340
All in all, it seems that there are advantages to both physical books and audiobooks.
总而言之，纸质书和有声书各有千秋。

18
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:30,740
Perhaps, next time you find yourself browsing bookshop shelves, also consider the format. It may just change your whole literary experience.
或许下次你在书店书架前流连时，可以多留意一下书籍的载体形式，它说不定会彻底改变你的阅读体验。
